{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}{{ 'field.arrange.title'|trans({'%shape%': shape.label}) }}{% endblock %}

{#
	The third door: the four things that are about the form rather than about a
	field ([XIV-163]).

	Order, width, which heading a field sits under and whether the list shows it.
	Every one of them is decided by looking at the *other* fields: a position
	means nothing except relative to the rest, two half-width fields share a row,
	a section is a run of them, and a column is worth having only against the
	columns already there. That is why this page still looks like the table the
	old editor was: it is the part of that table which genuinely wanted every
	field visible at once, with everything that did not want that taken out of
	it.

	One form for the whole shape, and one save. The alternative, a save per row,
	would be asking somebody to decide the order of a form one field at a time,
	which is the arrangement this page exists to stop.

	### The order is dragged, and the number went hidden ([XIV-165])

	It used to be typed, in tens, so that 15 put something between 10 and 20.
	That is a thing people know how to do and it was still the wrong instrument,
	because arranging a form is a spatial act and the number was a translation
	the customer was performing on the engine's behalf. Twenty-five fields is
	where that stops being a small favour.

	**What changed is the instrument and nothing else.** `position` is still an
	integer in tens (§5.1) and is still what the save reads; the rows below still
	carry one input each, now hidden, and a drop rewrites them 10, 20, 30 down
	the table before anything is sent. So one gesture is still zero requests, the
	whole shape still leaves in one submit through `updateField()`, and every
	§5.4 refusal still runs on the way in. A write per drop would have been a
	request per gesture and an unsaved state that can half-apply, which is the
	one thing this page was built not to be.

	This is also where the old note comes due. Typing beat move-up and move-down
	when each press was a form submission; since Live Components landed (§8.3)
	a press is a DOM move costing nothing, so the buttons came back beside the
	handle rather than instead of it, and they are the keyboard's path through
	the same mechanism.
#}
{% block body %}
	{{ include('_topbar.html.twig') }}

	<main class="container page-shell">
		{{ include('_flashes.html.twig') }}

		<div class="d-flex align-items-center justify-content-between mb-3">
			<div>
				<h1 class="h3 mb-0">{{ 'field.arrange.title'|trans({'%shape%': shape.label}) }}</h1>
				<p class="text-body-secondary small mb-0">{{ 'field.arrange.intro'|trans }}</p>
			</div>
			<div class="d-flex align-items-center gap-2">
				{#
					The headings on this module's form, on a page of their own (XIV-119),
					and reached from here because this is the page that needs them: a
					section is a thing with a name and a place, and something has to be
					able to make one before any field can be put in it. What this page
					draws is the select saying which heading each field is under, which is
					instantaneous and reversible and therefore fits a cell.

					Only on a module. A collection's rows are drawn as a table on the
					record page, and a table row has nowhere to put a heading.
				#}
				{% if shape.key == module.key %}
					<a class="btn btn-outline-secondary btn-sm" href="{{ path('field_sections', {module: module.key}) }}">
						{{ 'field.sections.link'|trans({'%count%': module.sections|length}) }}
					</a>
				{% endif %}
				<a class="btn btn-link" href="{{ path('field_index', {module: module.key}) }}">{{ 'field.options.back'|trans }}</a>
			</div>
		</div>

		<form method="post" action="{{ path('field_arrange_save', {module: module.key, shape: shape.id}) }}">
			<input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ csrf_token('edit-fields') }}">

			{#
				Whether there is anything to drag *between*. A collection has no
				sections and a module may have none yet, and in both cases the table
				is one flat run with no heading rows in it: there is no group to
				join, so drawing an "under no heading" row over every field would be
				a label for a distinction that does not exist here.
			#}
			{% set headed = shape.key == module.key and sections|length > 0 %}
			{% set columns = shape.key == module.key ? 6 : 5 %}

			{#
				The two sentences the announcement is made out of, translated here
				and filled in there. `|trans` with no parameters leaves `%field%` and
				the rest standing, which is what makes this work: the words are the
				server's, in the reader's language, and the controller only puts a
				name and two numbers into holes somebody who speaks the language
				chose the position of. Building the sentence in JavaScript would put
				German word order in an English file.
			#}
			<div class="card mb-3"
			     data-controller="arrange-fields"
			     data-arrange-fields-moved-value="{{ 'field.arrange.moved'|trans }}"
			     data-arrange-fields-moved-into-value="{{ 'field.arrange.moved_into'|trans }}">
				<div class="card-body">
					<div class="table-responsive">
						<table class="table table-sm align-middle mb-0 arrange-table">
							<thead>
								<tr>
									<th scope="col" class="text-body-secondary small text-uppercase">{{ 'common.name'|trans }}</th>
									<th scope="col" class="text-body-secondary small text-uppercase">{{ 'field.type'|trans }}</th>
									<th scope="col" class="text-body-secondary small text-uppercase">{{ 'field.order'|trans }}</th>
									<th scope="col" class="text-body-secondary small text-uppercase">{{ 'field.width'|trans }}</th>
									{% if shape.key == module.key %}
										<th scope="col" class="text-body-secondary small text-uppercase">{{ 'field.sections.column'|trans }}</th>
									{% endif %}
									<th scope="col" class="text-body-secondary small text-uppercase">{{ 'field.rule_listed'|trans }}</th>
								</tr>
							</thead>
							{#
								One `tbody` and one sortable list, headings included.

								The alternative, a list per section, reads better in the markup
								and cannot be dropped into: a run with no fields in it has no
								height, so the empty section somebody has just made would be the
								one place a field could not go. Here a heading is an ordinary
								row that simply has no handle on it, so it cannot be picked up
								and is still something a field can be dropped underneath.
							#}
							<tbody data-arrange-fields-target="list">
								{% for group in groups %}
									{% if headed %}
										{#
											The anchor for a run, and what a drop past it means. A field
											that comes to rest below this row is in this section, which is
											read off the DOM rather than tracked, so the select in the row
											and the heading above it cannot say different things.

											A heading does not move. Where a *section* sits is stored on
											the module rather than on any field (§5.4), and this form
											saves through `updateField()` per field and has no business
											writing the module row; that order is set on the sections
											page, which is one click away in the corner above.
										#}
										<tr class="arrange-heading" data-arrange-fields-target="heading"
										    data-section="{{ group.section ? group.section.key : '' }}">
											<th colspan="{{ columns }}" scope="colgroup" class="bg-body-tertiary">
												{% if group.section %}
													{{ group.section.label }}
												{% else %}
													<span class="text-body-secondary fst-italic">{{ 'field.arrange.no_heading'|trans }}</span>
												{% endif %}
											</th>
										</tr>
									{% endif %}
									{% for field in group.fields %}
										<tr data-arrange-fields-target="row" data-field-label="{{ field.label }}">
											<td>
												<a href="{{ path('field_edit', {module: module.key, field: field.id}) }}">{{ field.label }}</a>
												<div class="text-body-secondary small"><code>{{ field.key }}</code></div>
											</td>
											<td class="text-body-secondary">{{ types[field.type].label|default(field.type) }}</td>
											{#
												Where the field sits, as three things that are one thing: a
												handle for a mouse or a finger, two buttons for a keyboard,
												and the number both of them write.

												The number is hidden rather than gone. It is still what the
												save reads and still an integer in tens, so nothing about how
												a definition is stored moved; what it stopped being is a
												control, because the row's place in this table already says
												everything it said and says it in the shape of the answer.

												The handle is `aria-hidden`: a screen reader announcing a
												grip somebody cannot grip is worse than silence, and the two
												buttons beside it carry the same ability under a name. Each
												is labelled with its own field, so "move up" heard out of
												context still says which row it would move.
											#}
											<td>
												<div class="d-flex align-items-center gap-1">
													<span class="arrange-handle" aria-hidden="true" title="{{ 'field.arrange.drag'|trans }}">
														<i class="bi bi-grip-vertical"></i>
													</span>
													<button type="button" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-secondary arrange-nudge"
													        data-action="arrange-fields#up"
													        aria-label="{{ 'field.arrange.move_up'|trans({'%field%': field.label}) }}">
														<i class="bi bi-arrow-up" aria-hidden="true"></i>
													</button>
													<button type="button" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-secondary arrange-nudge"
													        data-action="arrange-fields#down"
													        aria-label="{{ 'field.arrange.move_down'|trans({'%field%': field.label}) }}">
														<i class="bi bi-arrow-down" aria-hidden="true"></i>
													</button>
													<input type="hidden" name="position[{{ field.id }}]" value="{{ field.position }}"
													       data-arrange-fields-target="position">
												</div>
											</td>
											{# How wide, in twelfths (XIV-43). Blank is the useful answer for
											   almost every field: it follows whatever that kind of field
											   usually wants, and keeps following it. #}
											<td>
												<select class="form-select form-select-sm" name="width[{{ field.id }}]"
												        style="width: 7rem" aria-label="{{ 'field.width'|trans }}">
													<option value="">{{ 'field.width_default'|trans }}</option>
													{% for n in 1..12 %}
														<option value="{{ n }}" {{ field.width == n ? 'selected' : '' }}>{{ n }}/12</option>
													{% endfor %}
												</select>
											</td>
											{#
												Which heading this field is drawn under (XIV-119). Blank is a
												real answer and the common one: a field in no section is drawn
												at the top of the form, exactly where every field in this
												product was drawn before sections existed.

												Kept beside the drag rather than replaced by it ([XIV-165]),
												and the two are wired together: dragging past a heading sets
												this, and choosing here moves the row under that heading. One
												of them is the gesture and the other is the way to send a
												field to a section nine rows away without nine presses, which
												is the same argument the number used to make and the reason a
												keyboard is not left with only the nudges.
											#}
											{% if shape.key == module.key %}
												<td>
													<select class="form-select form-select-sm" name="section[{{ field.id }}]"
													        style="width: 10rem" aria-label="{{ 'field.sections.column'|trans }}"
													        data-arrange-fields-target="section"
													        data-action="arrange-fields#reseat">
														<option value="">{{ 'field.sections.none'|trans }}</option>
														{% for section in sections %}
															<option value="{{ section.key }}" {{ field.section == section.key ? 'selected' : '' }}>{{ section.label }}</option>
														{% endfor %}
													</select>
												</td>
											{% endif %}
											{#
												Whether the list shows this field as a column. A checkbox sends
												nothing when it is unticked, which the save reads as the column
												being turned off. Safe here and nowhere else, because this
												form draws one for every field of the shape.
											#}
											<td>
												<input type="checkbox" name="listed[{{ field.id }}]" value="1"
												       {{ field.listed ? 'checked' : '' }} aria-label="{{ 'field.rule_listed'|trans }}">
											</td>
										</tr>
									{% endfor %}
								{% endfor %}
							</tbody>
						</table>
					</div>
					{#
						What to do, said on the page rather than left to be discovered.
						A grip is a picture and the arrows beside it are two more, so
						the sentence that says a keyboard reaches all of this is the
						only place anybody who cannot see them finds out. The second
						half is drawn only where there are headings to drop between,
						because a rule about sections on a table that has none is a
						rule about something not on the screen.
					#}
					<p class="text-body-secondary small mb-0 mt-2">
						{{ 'field.arrange.note'|trans }}
						{% if headed %}{{ 'field.arrange.note_sections'|trans }}{% endif %}
					</p>
					{#
						Where a move is said out loud. A DOM node changing parents tells
						a screen reader nothing at all, so every move, dragged or
						nudged, writes one sentence here naming the field and where it
						now is. This is the only thing on the page that exists to be
						read rather than seen.
					#}
					<p class="visually-hidden" aria-live="polite" data-arrange-fields-target="announcement"></p>
				</div>
			</div>

			<button class="btn btn-primary" type="submit">{{ 'common.save'|trans }}</button>
		</form>
	</main>
{% endblock %}
